PRIVATE REFLECTION: The undisputed core from the rat alcohol-preference model is:
* ~15% / 15.2% prefer alcohol, matching human prevalence;
* amygdala gene expression is broadly down in alcohol-preferring rats and Slc6a11 is down;
* Slc6a11 = GAT-3, Na+/Cl– GABA re-uptake transporter;
* paired-pulse PS at 40 µA: alcohol-preferring –0.38 mV vs sucrose-preferring –0.17 mV; shSlc6a11 –0.37 mV vs scrambled –0.16 mV. Loss of Slc6a11 phenocopies the alcohol-preferring electrophysiology.

Loss of GAT-3 → ↓ GABA clearance → ↑ ambient/extracellular GABA. That molecular inference is agreed by all.

The split is the interpretation of the PS change and its link to tonic GABA_A inhibition and pharmacology.

Physical amplitude reading: |PS| larger in alcohol-preferring / shSlc6a11 = larger net evoked principal cell output = increased amygdala excitability / neuronal activity. Up ambient GABA with increased |PS| is the classic chronic high ambient GABA → extrasynaptic GABA_A desensitisation/down-regulation paradox. Functional tonic inhibition is therefore reduced in alcohol-preferring / Slc6a11-low amygdala despite higher [GABA]o: sucrose-preferring > alcohol-preferring functional tonic inhibition. Net hyperexcitability would be normalised by restoring GABA_A tone, i.e. a GABA_A agonist may help addiction. This is the F / desensitisation account, argued by Colleagues 1,11,13,15,18,19,2,8,9 and the “None of the above” meta position.

Classic uptake → tone reading: authors are taken to read a larger/more negative PS as larger tonic GABAergic drive / decreased activity. Then ↓ Slc6a11 → ↑ extracellular GABA → ↑ tonic extrasynaptic GABA_A activation → increased tonic inhibition in alcohol-preferring vs sucrose-preferring, GABA_A receptors more active, extracellular GABA higher, and a GABA_A agonist would further mimic the alcohol-induced inhibitory state and reduce self-administration. This is the C/D/G account, argued by Colleagues 10,12,14,16,17,20,21,4,5,7.

Common problems noted:
* Options B,C,G contain the copy-typing error “compared to alcohol-preferring inhibition”;
* B/G couple increased neuronal activity / larger |PS| with increased tonic inhibition and higher extracellular GABA in the same animal;
* E inverts the Slc6a11 → extracellular GABA inference;
* The sign/magnitude convention for PS is unresolved: –0.38 mV is numerically smaller than –0.17 mV but |PS| is larger. Whether a larger |PS| means decreased neuronal activity / increased inhibition or increased excitability decides which set is coherent.

Unresolved: authors’ explicit sign convention for PS and whether they equate larger |PS| with increased inhibition. Without clarification the data are formally incompatible with a straight ↑GABA→↑inhibition→↓spike interpretation, making the “None of the above” position defensible verbatim. Mechanistically the closest internally coherent description respecting both the Slc6a11→↑[GABA]o phenocopy and the usual physical meaning of |PS| as excitability is the desensitisation/functional disinhibition account summarised in F: extracellular GABA higher in alcohol-preferring rats, functional tonic inhibition greater in sucrose-preferring than alcohol-preferring amygdala, net amygdala excitability increased, and GABA_A agonist as normalising therapy.

